  "plan": "First, inspect the environment to see what's already installed (git, sshd, nginx, openssl). Based on that, install missing packages. Then I'll build the rest of the setup: a 'user' account, bare git repo at /git/server, a post-receive hook that extracts the webserver branch into /var/www/html, an HTTPS-capable nginx on 8080, and SSH server. Finally I'll test the whole workflow locally (clone, commit, push, curl) to verify it produces 'hello world'.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/os-release 2>/dev/null | head -3\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "for c in git sshd nginx openssl ssh-keygen python3; do printf '%s: ' $c; command -v $c || echo MISSING; done\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

{
  "analysis": "Ubuntu 24.04. Only openssl and python3 are present. I need git, sshd (openssh-server), and nginx. I'll install them, then build the git server with a post-receive deploy hook and an HTTPS nginx server on 8080.",
  "plan": "Install the required packages non-interactively (git, openssh-server, nginx, ca-certificates). Then I'll configure: a 'user' account, a bare repo at /git/server with a post-receive hook that deploys the 'webserver' branch to /var/www/html via `git archive`, an sshd service, a self-signed TLS cert (trusted by the system) for nginx on 8080, and add 'server' to /etc/hosts. I'll do the install first and poll for completion.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get update\n",
      "duration": 30.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

{
  "analysis": "apt-get update completed successfully. Now I need to install git, openssh-server, and nginx.",
  "plan": "Install git, openssh-server, nginx, and ca-certificates. This may take a bit, so I'll allow time and poll for completion.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y git openssh-server nginx ca-certificates\n",
      "duration": 40.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

{
  "analysis": "The apt install failed: not enough free space in /var/cache/apt/archives. I need to investigate disk usage and free up space before installing. This is a constrained environment (the patch history hints at prior disk-space checks). Let me check available space and find large files to clean.",
  "plan": "Check disk space with df -h, look at what's consuming space under /var, /usr, /tmp, etc., and find large files (>5M) to remove. Then retry the install. I'll avoid touching anything important.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /usr /var /opt /root /home /tmp /app /etc 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

{
  "analysis": "The install failed only because apt's disk-space pre-check (df) reports the overlay filesystem as 100% full (Avail 0). But `du` shows only ~180M of actual files; the 957G used is the shared read-only base image. This is likely a fuse-overlayfs reporting artifact. I need to verify whether the writable layer actually has space, and clean/free space if needed before retrying the install.",
  "plan": "Investigate: check inode usage, confirm the overlay mount, and test whether I can actually write a file to the writable layer. Then look for large files to remove. If the overlay genuinely has no space, I'll use a tmpfs for the apt download cache as a workaround. Let me gather info first.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -i / ; mount | grep -i overlay\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/testfile bs=1M count=10 2>&1; echo EXIT=$?; rm -f /tmp/testfile\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "find /var /tmp /root -type f -size +5M 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

{
  "analysis": "The root cause is clear: the overlay's backing disk reports 0 available to unprivileged users because ~5% (≈49G) is reserved for root, while only ~27G is genuinely free. `apt` drops to the `_apt` user (sandbox) to check free space via statvfs f_bavail, sees 0, and aborts with 'not enough free space in /var/cache/apt/archives'. I verified I can actually write files to / (10MB dd succeeded), so the space is real — it's purely a reserved-blocks statvfs reporting issue. The fix: mount a tmpfs over the apt archives directory so the cache-space check sees genuine free space. If apt also checks the target filesystem, I'll handle it then.",
  "plan": "Mount a 1.5G tmpfs over /var/cache/apt/archives so apt's free-space pre-check passes, then retry the install. If it then complains about the install target (/), I'll address dpkg's space check. Let me mount and re-run the install.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mount -t tmpfs -o size=1536M tmpfs /var/cache/apt/archives\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h /var/cache/apt/archives\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get -o APT::Sandbox::User=root install -y git openssh-server nginx ca-certificates\n",
      "duration": 45.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

{
  "analysis": "The mount of tmpfs failed (no CAP_SYS_ADMIN in this container), so I can't change where the apt cache physically lives via mount. But I proved root CAN write to the overlay (the 10MB dd succeeded). The only blocker is apt's free-space guard, which uses statvfs f_bavail — and for this filesystem f_bavail is 0 because ~49G is reserved for root (5% of 984G) while only ~27G is free, so f_bavail = 27G - 49G reported as 0. The actual install target and cache space checks both read that 0. /dev/shm is a real tmpfs with ~64M genuinely free, so if I point apt's download cache there, the cache-space guard should pass. I'll test by installing one package first to validate, then proceed.",
  "plan": "Point apt's archives cache at /dev/shm (a tmpfs with real free space) via -o Dir::Cache::archives. Try installing 'git' alone first to confirm the space guard is satisfied and dpkg can unpack to /. If the combined approach works, install openssh-server and nginx. I'll use --no-install-recommends to minimize download size to fit /dev/shm's 64M. Monitor the result before proceeding.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /dev/shm/aptcache\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get -o Dir::Cache::archives=/dev/shm/aptcache install -y --no-install-recommends git 2>&1 | tail -25\n",
      "duration": 25.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

